            "contentType": "VIDEO",
            "title": "European luxury shares rise as creative debuts boost sales hopes",
            "description": "<title><body><p>STORY: Shares of European luxury brands rose Tuesday, pushing the industry\u2019s index to its highest level since May.</p><p>Designing debuts at fashion houses and fresh pushes for affordability have given investors hope the sector could have a comeback.</p><p>The rally was led by French majors.</p><p>Kering, Christian Dior and LVMH rose between 3% and 7% in the early afternoon.\u00a0</p><p>Luxury groups in Europe have tried to turn around a difficult period, where U.S. tariffs and slower demand in China have hit profits.</p><p>:: File</p><p>New designers at houses of Gucci, Bottega Veneta and Dior presented their collections at last week's Milan Fashion week.</p><p>One analyst said investors were \u201cvery pleased\u201d with the new collections\u2026</p><p>Adding that some luxury brands are reworking price structures to get more affordable products onto shelves.\u00a0</p><p>Morgan Stanley analysts have also bet on what it calls a \"burst of creativity\u201d in the industry.\u00a0</p><p>They upgraded\u00a0recommendations for Kering and LVMH in a note to investors.</p></body></title>",
            "summary": "<body><p>STORY: Shares of European luxury brands rose Tuesday, pushing the industry\u2019s index to its highest level since May.</p><p>Designing debuts at fashion houses and fresh pushes for affordability have given investors hope the sector could have a comeback.</p><p>The rally was led by French majors.</p><p>Kering, Christian Dior and LVMH rose between 3% and 7% in the early afternoon.&nbsp;</p><p>Luxury groups in Europe have tried to turn around a difficult period, where U.S. tariffs and slower demand in China have hit profits.</p><p>:: File</p><p>New designers at houses of Gucci, Bottega Veneta and Dior presented their collections at last week's Milan Fashion week.</p><p>One analyst said investors were \u201cvery pleased\u201d with the new collections\u2026</p><p>Adding that some luxury brands are reworking price structures to get more affordable products onto shelves.&nbsp;</p><p>Morgan Stanley analysts have also bet on what it calls a \"burst of creativity\u201d in the industry.&nbsp;</p><p>They upgraded&nbsp;recommendations for Kering and LVMH in a note to investors.</p></body>",
